{
  "dom_id": "step-q126156",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{9}(x-1)(x-4),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$",
  "worked_solution": "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $1\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}-\\frac{2}{9}(t-1)(t-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}-\\frac{2}{9}({t}^{2}-5t+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2}{27}{t}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{t}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}t)|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x-(-\\frac{2}{27}+\\frac{5}{9}-\\frac{8}{9}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [
